WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.760
have you ever wanted to import python&nbsp;
code directly from the internet

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.200
like from a GitHub repo?

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.320
well now you can using the magic of cloud Imports

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.120
forget the fuss of creating a requirements&nbsp;
txt and installing your dependencies

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:23.000
just import cloud_imports add your GitHub or other&nbsp;
remote repository and whamo bamo it just works

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.600
this is the real deal folks

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.160
I haven't secretly pre-installed this&nbsp;
prime number library in the background

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.000
if I remove the repo from cloud&nbsp;
imports i get an import error

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000
put it back and we're back in business

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.400
there are absolutely no downsides to&nbsp;
running code directly from the Internet

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.360
it's fast it's efficient it's bleeding edge&nbsp;
and there are no security risks whatsoever

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.520
so for the low low price of $39.99 a month ...

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.480
okay hopefully by now you've&nbsp;
caught on that this is a joke and

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.280
importing code directly from&nbsp;
the internet at runtime is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.840
at best a horribly horribly misguided idea

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.520
and at worst a complete&nbsp;
performance and security nightmare

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.360
but this code is real

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.800
it works it actually downloads&nbsp;
files from GitHub at runtime

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.240
dynamically creates modules&nbsp;
and runs the correct code

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.760
you can use the inspect&nbsp;
module to see the source code

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.800
and to your boss's horror if you ask for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.920
module's file it gives you the URL&nbsp;
where the file was downloaded from

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.720
so the real question for this&nbsp;
video is "excuse me, what??"

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:34.240
and to answer this very articulate question&nbsp;
we need to learn about Python's import system

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.720
you're no doubt already familiar&nbsp;
with how imports normally work

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.680
but did you know that Python's import&nbsp;
system is accessible from python itself

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.120
and it allows you to customize&nbsp;
what the import statement does

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.000
this is the purpose of the built-in importlib

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.960
to understand importlib there's&nbsp;
just three things you need to know:

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.920
finders, loaders, and the system meta path

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.680
the overview is that you register&nbsp;
finders in your meta path

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.760
and python tries each finder in&nbsp;
turn until either a finder succeeds

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.400
or it runs out of finders in which&nbsp;
case you'd get an import error

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.120
if a finder succeeds it gives&nbsp;
a specification for the module

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.640
that includes the loader that should&nbsp;
be used to actually create the module

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.120
then python uses the loader to&nbsp;
create and initialize the module

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.240
take a look at the meta path as it is now

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.680
the first item in the meta&nbsp;
path is a built-in importer

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.440
which is used for finding modules built into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.680
the python interpreter itself&nbsp;
like itertools, sys, and time

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.800
then there's this frozen importer which is used in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.440
cases where the interpreter&nbsp;
is embedded into a binary

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.720
like if you're using pyinstaller&nbsp;
you can ignore that for this video

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:40.240
and the third path finder one implements the usual&nbsp;
logic looking in directories in the system path

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.920
which is how python would find&nbsp;
modules in the current directory

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.320
modules installed into your virtual env

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:49.320
and built-in modules that were written in&nbsp;
Python rather than compiled into the interpreter

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.400
like the json or inspect modules

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.880
all we have to do to get cloud imports&nbsp;
is to add another finder to the meta path

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.640
as a fallback for when python&nbsp;
wouldn't find the module any other way

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.480
that's all this add repo function does,&nbsp;
add another entry to the meta path

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.120
finders just need to implement&nbsp;
one method "find_spec"

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.800
which takes the dotted name of the module

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.560
and needs to return a module&nbsp;
spec if it can find the module

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.400
or None to let the next finder&nbsp;
in the meta path have a try

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.120
a module spec just holds the loader and some extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.840
info like the module name and&nbsp;
whether this module is a package

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.520
we implement the cloud finder by replacing&nbsp;
dots in Python imports with slashes in URLs

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.960
and appending that onto the base URL&nbsp;
that the finder was constructed with

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.640
like the GitHub repo URL

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.480
we go out to the internet and download those&nbsp;
URLs and see if we find valid python source

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:42.360
we check both for ".py" files as well as&nbsp;
for "__init__.py" files one directory down

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:48.000
so that cloud imports work with multiple files and&nbsp;
packages as long as they're written in pure python

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.320
if we find a valid python file at one of the&nbsp;
URLs then we return a spec for the module

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.920
that has a custom cloud loader&nbsp;
holding the downloaded source code

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.720
in the loader we need to implement&nbsp;
"create_module" and "exec_module"

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.800
which are kind of like "__new__"&nbsp;
and "__init__" but for modules

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:08.160
in create module we create a blank module&nbsp;
and add it to the system module cache

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.960
or we get it from the module&nbsp;
cache if it already exists

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.080
in exec module we initialize the&nbsp;
module by executing the source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.480
code we downloaded within the module's dictionary

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.000
this "get_source" isn't required but it allows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.520
inspect module to find the&nbsp;
source of the module as well

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:26.680
and that's it

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:31.840
add some convenience functions for adding&nbsp;
different kinds of repos and you're set

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.200
zooming out it's surprisingly little code

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.440
under 100 lines to completely&nbsp;
change how python imports work

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:41.760
again cloud imports are a mind-bogglingly bad idea

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.360
but I think understanding how&nbsp;
to write a cloud importer really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.680
helps you understand how the import system works

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.120
and in particular how customizable it actually is

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.400
see the import lib docs for more

